@startuml Jenkins CI/CD
' !theme plain

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

actor "Developer" as Developer
participant "GitHub\nRepository" as GitHub
participant "Jenkins\nCI Server\n(AWS EC2 VM)" as Jenkins
participant "Docker Daemon\n(AWS EC2 VM)" as DockerD
database "Docker Hub\nRegistry" as DockerHub

Developer -> GitHub : 1. git push to 'main'
activate GitHub

GitHub -> Jenkins : 2. Webhook triggers HTTP POST\n(Push Event)
deactivate GitHub
activate Jenkins

Jenkins -> GitHub : 3. Git pull/checkout (Fetch Jenkinsfile & source)
GitHub --> Jenkins : Source code delivered

Jenkins -> DockerD : 4. Execute: `docker build -t verket/docker-hello-world:latest .`\n(via /var/run/docker.sock)
activate DockerD
note right of DockerD
  Docker daemon on the EC2 machine
  reads the Dockerfile and compiles 
  the C++ code inside the container
end note
DockerD --> Jenkins : Image built locally on VM
deactivate DockerD

Jenkins -> DockerHub : 5. Execute: `docker login`\n(Authenticates with jenkins credentials)
activate DockerHub
DockerHub --> Jenkins : Login Succeeded
deactivate DockerHub

Jenkins -> DockerD : 6. Execute: `docker push verket/docker-hello-world:latest`
activate DockerD
DockerD -> DockerHub : 7. Uploads image layers & manifest over network
activate DockerHub
DockerHub --> DockerD : Image accepted
deactivate DockerHub
DockerD --> Jenkins : Push completed successfully
deactivate DockerD

Jenkins -> DockerD : 8. Execute: `docker rmi`\n(Cleans up local VM disk space)
note right of Jenkins
  Jenkins clears the image from the 
  EC2 instance so it doesn't fill up
end note

Jenkins --> Developer : 9. Marks Pipeline as Success
deactivate Jenkins

@enduml
